    "correctAnswer": "A",
    "correctAnswerText": "Increased intracellular glial glutamine",
    "explanation": "The patient in the vignette has acute hepatic encephalopathy, likely from an undisclosed acetaminophen overdose, based on the time frame of her symptoms and her history of depression. The etiology of hepatic encephalopathy is multifactorial. The primary process involves astrocyte swelling leading to cerebral edema. Elevated ammonia and glutamate are converted by glutamine synthetase in brain astrocytes to glutamine, which is an osmole that draws fluid intracellularly. An alternative mechanism recently proposed suggests that in acute liver failure, newly synthesized glutamine becomes trapped within the astrocyte as a consequence of down-regulation of its high affinity glutamine transporter SNATS. Therefore, restricted transfer of glutamine out of the cell rather than increased synthesis within the cell could potentially explain the cell swelling/brain edema seen in acute liver failure. Increased stimulation of GABA receptors on neurons and astrocytes also contributes to cell swelling. For this reason, drugs that increase GABA-ergic stimulation of the central nervous system (CNS), such as benzodiazepines, are relatively contraindicated in these patients. Increased neuronal ammonia, which may be present, is not directly responsible for the cerebral edema. Uremia can also be present in these patients, as concomitant renal failure is not uncommon, but they are not as important as increased intracellular glutamine to the pathogenesis of hepatic encephalopathy. Increased oxygen free radical production is thought to play a role in hypoxic-ischemic reperfusion brain injury.",
