    "correctAnswerText": "Thyroid-stimulating hormone",
    "explanation": "Patients with trisomy 21 are at higher risk for congenital hypothyroidism that may not be fully evident at birth and is therefore missed on newborn screening. The slightly low sodium, elevated potassium, low-normal calcium, and elevated phosphorus are all consistent with delayed onset of hypothyroidism. Also, symptoms such as poor appetite, macroglossia, and decreased tone could all be attributed to his underlying genetic defect, which can contribute to delayed diagnosis. Brain natriuretic peptide helps diagnose heart failure, which could be a possibility in a child with unrepaired atrial septal defect. However, the description of only mildly depressed function without right-to-left shunting makes it unlikely that his decreased tone is due to heart failure. Botulism should always be considered in a child with hypotonia and especially one with constipation and who has been fed solids before age 6 months. Infants are at higher risk for botulism since they have lower amounts of gastric acid and normal flora, along with immature immune systems that allow the bacteria to produce the toxin. However, the test for botulin toxin is performed by the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention and takes at least 24 hours to run, during which time the patient may progress to respiratory failure and require ventilator support. If botulism is suspected, the antitoxin should be administered as quickly as possible. Urine toxicology screening can help assess for accidental ingestions of medications and poisons but the stable vital signs and lack of respiratory depression make opioid or sedative ingestion unlikely.",
