    "correctAnswerText": "Cefepime and vancomycin",
    "explanation": "This patient presents with febrile neutropenia (absolute neutrophil count less than or equal to 500 cells/mL?) with evidence of septic shock (tachycardia, hypotension, and elevated blood lactate level). He is at high risk for serious life-threatening infections due to severe neutropenia. Based on the most current guidelines for the management of fever and neutropenia in children, most pediatric patients with febrile neutropenia who are stable can be empirically treated with monotherapy with an antipseudomonal beta-lactam or a carbapenem. The most appropriate antibiotic coverage for this patient is a combination of antipseudomonal beta-lactam (piperacillin-tazobactam or ticarcillin-clavulanic acid), antipseudomonal cephalosporins (cefepime or ceftazidime) or a carbapenem (meropenem or imipenem) and addition of glycopeptide (vancomycin). Initiation of empiric antifungal therapy should be considered in patients who are at high risk for invasive fungal infection, including those with acute myeloid leukemia or relapsed acute leukemia, those on highly myelosuppressive chemotherapy for other malignancies, and allogeneic hematopoietic stem cell transplant recipients with persistent fever despite prolonged (greater than or equal to 4 days) broad-spectrum antibiotic therapy and expected prolonged neutropenia (greater than or equal to 10 days).",
