    "correctAnswerText": "Euthyroid sick syndrome",
    "explanation": "Thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) from the anterior pituitary modulates thyroid hormone synthesis. TSH facilitates the sodium iodide symported at the thyroid follicular cells, resulting in enhanced iodine concentration in the thyroid gland, increased synthesis of thyroglobulin, and activation of thyroid peroxidase. The protein thyroglobulin is synthesized within the rough endoplasmic reticulum and packed in the Golgi apparatus, forming the thyroglobulin secretory vesicles released into the follicular cell lumen. Thyroid oxidase produces hydrogen peroxide, subsequently used by thyroid peroxidase to oxidize iodide to iodine. Thyroid peroxidase also catalyzes the coupling of adjacent iodotyrosines to form thyroxine (T4), and a lesser amount of triiodothyronine (T3). In the peripheral tissues, T4 is metabolized to T3 and reverse T3 (rT3). Euthyroid sick syndrome, common among critically ill patients, is a transient thyroid dysregulation characterized by a rapid decrease in T3 and variable increase in rT3. This response is proportional to the intensity of critical illness. As patients recover from euthyroid sick syndrome, serum TSH levels may transiently rise into the 10-20 mU/L range. A TSH level above 20 mU/L almost always indicates true primary hypothyroidism.",
