    "correctAnswerText": "Its interrater reliability is high.",
    "explanation": "Screening tests should have high disease validity as well as interrater reliability, the likelihood that different clinicians administering the test will obtain the same result. Tests should be cost effective and easy to administer by clinicians with minimal training. The disease or condition being screened for should be a common one. The Cornell Assessment of Pediatric Delirium (CAPD) screens for pediatric delirium, which is common in pediatric ICU patients. The CAPD can easily be administered by clinicians with minimal training. It has high interrater reliability and high validity for delirium assessment.",
