    "explanation": "Absolute contraindications to extracorporeal life support (ECLS) include lethal chromosomal abnormalities (trisomy 13 or 18), severe neurologic compromise, uncontrolled bleeding, incurable malignancy, prematurity of less than 30 weeks, and weight less than 1 kg (2.2 lb). Relative contraindications include prolonged pre-ECLS mechanical ventilation (> 14 days), especially at high ventilator settings, recent (within 1 week) neurosurgical procedure or intracranial hemorrhage, preexisting illness with poor long-term prognosis, history of allogenic bone marrow transplant, or solid organ tumors. The patient with the open-book pelvic fracture, hemodynamic instability, and critically low hemoglobin has hemorrhagic shock, and ECLS with associated anticoagulation is contraindicated. ECLS is also contraindicated in the patient with acute myeloblastic leukemia because of her history of allogenic bone marrow transplant and recurrent disease (poor long-term prognosis), as well as the premature infant (< 30 weeks) with trisomy 18 (lethal chromosomal abnormality). ECLS can provide lifesaving support for patients with severe pediatric acute respiratory distress syndrome secondary to respiratory viral infections, such as the 4-year-old boy with respiratory syncytial virus.",
